✅ What is JRE (Java Runtime Environment)?
JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment. It provides everything needed to run Java programs — but not develop them.

🔹 Key Points:
JRE = JVM + Core Java Libraries

It does not include tools like javac, javadoc, etc.

It’s mainly used by end-users or environments where you only need to run Java apps (not compile).

📦 JRE Contains:
| Component                                | Purpose                                                            |
| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **JVM (Java Virtual Machine)**           | Executes `.class` files (Java bytecode)                            |
| **Core Libraries** (`rt.jar` or modules) | Provides essential Java APIs (like `java.lang`, `java.util`, etc.) |
| **Deployment Libraries**                 | For running applets, Java Web Start (older)                        |
| **Property Files & Configs**             | Timezones, security policies, etc.                                 |


🧠 How JRE Works:

Java Bytecode (.class)
         ↓
       JRE
   ↙           ↘
JVM        Core Libraries
         ↓
    Program Runs
⚠️ Difference Between JDK and JRE:
| Feature            | **JDK**                | **JRE**            |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- | ------------------ |
| For                | Develop + Run          | Only Run           |
| Includes           | JRE + Compiler + Tools | JVM + Libraries    |
| Tools like `javac` | ✅ Yes                  | ❌ No               |
| Used By            | Developers             | End-users, servers |


✅ Real-World Use:
You install JDK to develop apps.

Your users install JRE (or it’s bundled in the app) to run those apps.

☑️ Since Java 11, Oracle no longer distributes a separate JRE — everything is part of the JDK.